data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider let you opt into **sync-over-async** execution (e.g., via `EnableSyncExecution()`). In .NET 11, **that opt-in is removed entirely**. Every synchronous LINQ operation on the Cosmos provider now throws `InvalidOperationException` unconditionally.

**All four of your methods break:**

| Sync (breaks) | Async (replacement) |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

## The fix

Convert to async all the way through:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- **Callers must be updated too** — `async` propagates up the call stack.
- `Add()` stays synchronous (it's an in-memory operation, not a query).
- Remove any `EnableSyncExecution()` / sync-over-async configuration — it no longer compiles.
